KW  - Digital sovereignty
KW  - Gaia-X
KW  - Platform governance
KW  - Regional integration
AB  - This chapter investigates the evolving concept of digital sovereignty through a comparative analysis of two regional integration projects: Europe's Gaia-X and China's Digital Silk Road (DSR). Drawing on Deleuze and Guattari's distinction between smooth and striated spaces, the chapter examines how sovereignty in the digital realm is enacted through the design of infrastructures, governance frameworks, and the regulation of data flows. While Gaia-X reflects a classical model of regional integration, grounded in institutional coordination and normative convergence among sovereign states, the DSR exemplifies an alternative, infrastructural mode of integration. Rather than relying on formal membership, the DSR consolidates transnational digital space through technological embedding, platform standardization, and the creation of asymmetric dependencies across participating countries. This infrastructural regionalism positions the DSR as a distinctive non-Western model of integration, expanding China's influence beyond its borders. The chapter argues that digital sovereignty today is less about territorial control and more about the capacity to assemble, manage, and govern interconnected digital environments, revealing competing strategies for balancing autonomy and interdependence in the emerging global digital order.
ER  - 

